{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Arizona Diamondbacks arrived as defending National League champions with championship expectations, but the season quickly revealed a team that would have to fight for every inch. After a promising spring, April turned into a nightmare — the D-backs went 11-16, battered by blown leads, bad bullpen outings, and an offense that couldn't find its footing when it mattered most. The ghosts of a magical 2023 run seemed to weigh on the roster, and early losses piled up faster than anyone anticipated.\n\nMay offered little relief at 11-15, but something began to shift beneath the surface. June cracked open like a dam breaking — the club went 16-11, with Ketel Marte delivering clutch home runs, the lineup finally clicking, and a sense of collective belief returning to the clubhouse. July was even better at 17-8, the best month of the season, powered by Joc Pederson's thunder, Gabriel Moreno's clutch doubles, and a lineup that could manufacture runs in every inning. The D-backs looked like a team finally playing to their ceiling.\n\nAugust was their best calendar month by wins at 18-9, a blistering pace that briefly made the playoffs feel inevitable. Pederson continued his torrid stretch, Adrian Del Castillo provided unexpected sparks off the bench, and even when the bullpen wobbled — Paul Sewald's struggles were a recurring theme throughout the summer — the offense bailed them out more often than not. The team was scoring runs in bunches, slugging 211 home runs on the year, and riding an identity as one of baseball's most dangerous offensive clubs.\n\nBut September told a cruel story. The D-backs went 13-13 down the stretch when they needed wins most, unable to separate themselves from the Wild Card pack. A finish that felt like destiny in late August fizzled into an 89-73 record — good enough in most years, not good enough in 2024. The Diamondbacks missed the playoffs, leaving a roster full of talent to spend a long winter reflecting on the April hole they could never fully escape.",
